Date: Sat, 17 Apr 1993 20:44:54 GMT

[...]

I've done some research on [...] the question "if a moderator is [at
a] government-site-based, what is his or her authority/right to
exclude material?"

Yesterday, I called Mark Goodman, Esq., of the Student Press Law
Center, 202 466-5242, 800 18th., NW, Suite 300, Washington, DC 20006.
He said that a student editor/moderator has pretty much an absolute
right to exclude. He referenced _Sinn v. Daily Nebraskan_ (1987), a
federal appellate decision. The court ruled that a student newspaper
editor at the U. of Nebraska had the right to exclude not just main
newspaper material, but even classified ads.

The rationale was the state can limit who can speak in created forums.
In the case of university newspapers the forum is limited to the
student editor(s).

As background, private editors have had this right-to-exclude
explicitly since _Miami Herald Publishing Co. v. Tornillo, a Supreme
Court decision.  Here is a summary of this case from _Congressional
Quarterly's guide to the U.S. Supreme Court_, 2nd Ed, Elder Witt,
editor, 1990:

   The Right of Reply

   In a decision handed down the following year, the Court emphasized
   the narrowness of the _Pittsburgh Press_ holding [forbiding
   sex-classifed help-wanted ads -cmk] when it struck down a Florida
   statute that required newspapers to grant political candidates
   equal space to reply to the paper's criticism of their public
   records.

   Writing for a unanimous Court in _Miami Herald Publishing Co. v.
   Tornillo_ (1974), Chief Justice Warren E. Burger carefully reviewed
   the arguments in favor of the law, acknowledging that the
   diminishing number of newspapers and the concentration of media
   ownership meant that frequently only one view of an issue was
   published. "Chains of newspapers, national newspapers, national
   wire and news services, and one-newspaper towns are the dominant
   features of a press that has become noncompetitive and enormously
   powerful and influential in its capacity to manipulate popular
   opinion and change the court of events," he wrote.

   But a government command to print specific information collides
   with the freedom of the press guaranteed by the First Amendment, he
   said, and under the decision of the Court

        any such compulsion to publish that which "'reason' tells them
        should not be published" is unconstitutional. A responsible
        press is an undoubtedly desirable goal, but press
        responsibility is not mandated by the Constitution and like
        many other virtues it cannot be legislated.

   In conclusion, Burger wrote:

        A newspaper is more than a passive receptacle or conduit for
        news, comment, and advertising. The choice of material to go
        into a newspaper, and the decision made as to limitations on
        the size and content of the paper, and the treatment of public
        issues and public official -- whether fair or unfair --
        constitute the exercise of editorial control and judgment. It
        has yet to be demonstrated how government regulation of this
        critical process can be exercised consistent with First
        Amendment guarantees of a free press as they have evolved to
        this time.

Mark Goldman also mentioned _Avins v. Rutgers_ (1968) as an important
case involving student editors. Here is a summary of the case from
_College Student Press Law_, 2nd edition, by Robert Trager and Donna
L.  Dickerson (1979):

    A case involving a law review substantiates a student editor's
    powers to select and reject material for publication. An editor of
    the _Rutgers University Law Review_ refused to print an article
    submitted by a law school professor who later claimed the
    rejection was based on the editor's disagreement with the
    article's ideology. The Third Circuit upheld the editor's right to
    exercise his editorial judgment, noting that more material was
    submitted to the review than could be published. The court said
    the article's author could not insist that his piece be published
    in preference to others the student editors deemed of higher quality.
    The fact that the review was financed in part with state funds did
    not require that its pages be open to all who wished to be
    represented in them.

The current edition of the Student Press Law Center's _Law of the
Student Press_ (1985 with 1988 additions) does not mention this issue
at all, but Mr. Goodman said the next edition, due out latter this
year will cover this issue. I asked if Bradley Smith, the guy trying
to buy advertising space for holocaust revisionism had sued. Mr. Goodman
said not that he knew of, probably because Mr. Smith knows that he
would loose.

As far as nonstudents moderators, Mr. Goodman said it depends. On the
one hand if a university employee manages a open forum, that employee
can't practice, for example, viewpoint discrimination. On the other
hand, although _Avins v. Rutgers_ involved a student editor, the judge
did not use that editor's student status in his reasoning. So, a
university-employee can probably have editorial rights, too. I
suggested that if an university-employed editor said up front that
articles will be selected by quality, relevance to topic, etc, in the
opinion of the editor, his or her judgments could not be second
guessed. Mr. Goodman concurred.

- Carl
